      "description": "Cream reading text on the full dark espresso material.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-copper-paper\"><section class=\"ds-copper-panel\"><h3>Qualifikationen</h3><p>Abschnittsinhalt aus dem CV</p></section></div>",
      "css": ".ds-copper-paper{padding:20px;background:#211c19;color:#fff3e4;font-family:\"DM Sans Variable\",\"DM Sans\",sans-serif}.ds-copper-panel{padding:22px;background:#2b2420;border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,#c38d65 28%,#342b25);border-radius:0}.ds-copper-panel h3{margin:0 0 12px;font-family:\"Instrument Serif\",Georgia,serif;font-size:26px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.13;letter-spacing:-.01em}.ds-copper-panel p{margin:0;color:#dfcfbf;font-size:12.3px;line-height:1.49}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Lumen asymmetric group",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "lumen-panel",
      "description": "The source 38/62 portfolio group; real content passes whole-group measurement before use.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-lumen-group\"><section class=\"ds-lumen-context\"><h3>Kurzprofil</h3><p>Kontext aus dem CV</p></section><section class=\"ds-lumen-main\"><h3>Berufserfahrung</h3><p>Hervorgehobener Eintrag</p></section><section class=\"ds-lumen-support\"><h3>Berufserfahrung</h3><p>Weiterer Eintrag</p></section></div>",
      "css": ".ds-lumen-group{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,.38fr) minmax(0,.62fr);grid-template-rows:auto auto;gap:18px 20px;padding:18px;background:#fefcff;color:#30263f;font-family:\"DM Sans Variable\",\"DM Sans\",sans-serif;font-size:12.3px;line-height:1.5}.ds-lumen-group h3{margin:0 0 13px;font-size:12px;font-weight:650;line-height:1.4;letter-spacing:.005em}.ds-lumen-group p{margin:0;color:#5a5066}.ds-lumen-context{grid-column:1;grid-row:1;padding:2px 0 0}.ds-lumen-main{grid-column:2;grid-row:1/span 2;padding:20px;border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,#8a70c7 14%,#e7e0ed);border-radius:8px;background:#fff}.ds-lumen-support{grid-column:1;grid-row:2;padding:17px 18px;border-radius:8px;background:color-mix(in srgb,#8a70c7 5%,#fbf8ff)}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "One real CV, ten document worlds",
    "overview": "The dark navy editor is a compact working studio around the real A4 document. Pale blue actions, tonal panels and fine dividers support editing, comparison and export. The documents carry the expressive design: rounded product boards, a square dashboard, an angular timeline, an open magazine, a poster, organic panels, espresso paper, a horizontal ledger, an asymmetric portfolio and an architectural split.\n\nThis is a descriptive record of the implemented V4 collection, grounded in the user's explicit request for ten genuinely different styles and deliberate second pages. The settled direction was assigned directly in code from the ten briefs. No V4 random FORM seed, user-selected metaphor or approved bitmap composition is claimed. The editor identity is retained; the former collection of similar two-column documents is superseded.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A restrained navy editor framing a large, fixed A4 artifact.",
      "Ten structural families with their own type, geometry and paper material.",
      "First and qualification pages use related but purposeful compositions.",
      "Real editable text, local fonts, Lucide SVG icons and uploaded portraits or factual initials.",
      "Content-aware measurement supports the same paper geometry in preview, public view and native PDF."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Accent Derivation Rule",
        "body": "Keep each world's paper, ink and material relationship when the accent changes. The source derives accent ink against white or Copper's espresso paper and chooses black or white for accent-filled text; world-specific mixes remain part of that world. This is implementation behavior, not a blanket contrast certification.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Display Identity Rule",
        "body": "Treat frontmatter name roles as default display values. Reading-font choices and each world's scoped overrides may change them; never flatten all ten identities into one global display rule.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Screen and Paper Rule",
        "body": "Screen shadows separate paper and editing layers from the studio. Printed composition comes from each world's actual rules, panels and materials; native export removes the surrounding screen paper shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do preserve stored template IDs while using current visible names.",
      "Do preserve real content and source order through every world, including additional roles and qualification continuations.",
      "Do measure actual frames, panel padding, borders, gaps and grouped grids before pagination.",
      "Do keep A4 geometry and local fonts consistent across preview, public view and native PDF.",
      "Do preserve inherited SVG colors, user photo shape, cleared fields and footer visibility.",
      "Do keep gallery page silhouettes aligned with their document families."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't reduce the collection to recolors or left/right swaps of one column layout.",
      "Don't cap custom CVs at two pages, globally shrink body text to force that count or truncate content.",
      "Don't add fabricated skills, levels, portraits, claims or decorative printed labels to fill space.",
      "Don't impose one radius, sidebar, display font or continuation composition on every world.",
      "Don't promote a task-specific arrangement, synthesized swatch ramp or this survey into a product-wide restriction or quality certification."
    ]
  }
}
